The easiest and most efficient way to change the color of a `<span>` on mouseover is using **CSS**. 

Here are the three most common ways to do this, ranging from the highly recommended CSS approach to JavaScript approaches.

---

### Method 1: CSS (Recommended)
This is the best practice. It is lightweight, performs better, and keeps your styling separate from your HTML structure. You use the `:hover` pseudo-class.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<style>
  /* Base style for the span */
  .hover-text {
    color: black;
    cursor: pointer; /* Changes the mouse cursor to a hand pointer */
    transition: color 0.3s ease; /* Optional: smooths out the color change transition */
  }

  /* Style when the mouse hovers over the span */
  .hover-text:hover {
    color: #ff4757; /* Changes color to red on hover */
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

  <p>This is a normal paragraph with a <span class="hover-text">hoverable span</span> inside it.</p>

</body>
</html>
```

---

### Method 2: Inline HTML/JavaScript (Quickest/Easiest)
If you only need to apply this to a single, quick element and don't want to write a separate CSS block, you can use the inline `onmouseover` and `onmouseout` attributes.

```html
<span 
  style="color: black; cursor: pointer;" 
  onmouseover="this.style.color='blue'" 
  onmouseout="this.style.color='black'">
  Hover over me!
</span>
```

---

### Method 3: JavaScript Event Listeners
If you need the color change to trigger other logic or if you are dynamically generating elements in JavaScript, you can use standard JavaScript event listeners.

```html
<span id="dynamic-span">Hover over me!</span>

<script>
  const mySpan = document.getElementById('dynamic-span');

  // Change color on mouse enter
  mySpan.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => {
    mySpan.style.color = 'green';
  });

  // Revert color on mouse leave
  mySpan.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
    mySpan.style.color = 'black';
  });
</script>
```

### Which one should you choose?
* Use **Method 1 (CSS)** for 99% of web development projects. It's clean, reusable, and allows you to add a smooth `transition` effect easily.
* Use **Method 2** only for quick prototyping or testing.
* Use **Method 3** if the color change depends on complex programmatic conditions in your JavaScript code.

